% Test: top-level type parameter in a procedure declaration
% EXPECT: well-typed (positive)
%
% Supersedes negative/type_def/merge_undefined_type.glp.
%
% A type parameter may occur bare at a top-level argument position (TGLP
% parameterized-types.tex: "each may occur bare or within a template
% instantiation anywhere in the argument types").  A carried value with no
% constructor wrapper and no common supertype — e.g. a lib/ router's message
% argument — can only appear this way.  The declaration names it in its
% parameter list ("Declaration parameters": "The parameters of a procedure
% declaration are exactly those its parameter list names"), so
% procedure(M) keep(M?, M) is a valid parameterised procedure.  Until
% 2026-09-27 it named none and M was inferred as a parameter, the transitional
% reading the paper replaced.
%
% This pins two things:
%   1. the top-level parameter M is accepted — the declaration loads;
%   2. a caller instantiating M at a concrete type type-checks — call-site
%      instantiation binds M = Stream(Integer).
% A known type at a top-level position stays concrete, so monomorphic
% declarations are unaffected.  Per-instantiation defining-clause checking
% (Phase 2, project mode) is pinned by the negative routing fixture.

procedure(M) keep(M?, M).
keep(X, X?).

procedure use_keep(Stream(Integer)?, Stream(Integer)).
use_keep(A, B?) :- keep(A?, B).
